## 3  Results and Discussion
## 3.1  Synthesis and Characterization of MOFs
The MIL-101 with POD-like characteristics, excellent stability and functional tunability was synthesized by using $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ as a coordination center and terephthalic acid as organic ligands [44], which is selected as the model to design the modified nanozymes. To regulate the electronic or microstructure of MOFs, the strong electronwithdrawing $-\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ and electron-donating $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups are introduced to synthesize functionalized MIL-101, respectively. The detailed synthetic procedures are presented in Supporting Information. It is established that the nodes of MIL-101 are constituted by three octahedra sharing a $\mu_{3}–0$ corner and one Fe at the center [45]. As shown in Fig. 1a–c, the theoretical models of the local structural unit of the different functionalized MIL-101 are the same, except that the $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ or $-\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ replaces the $-\mathrm{H}$ of terephthalic acid. First, the morphologies of the as-prepared materials were characterized by transmission electron microscopy (TEM). As can be seen in Fig. 1d–f, the MIL-101, $\mathbf{NO}_{2^{-}}\mathbf{MIL}.-101$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 show uniform octahedron morphologies with a diameter of about $600{-}800~\mathrm{nm}$ . High-angle annular dark-field scanning TEM (HADDF-STEM) image and its corresponding energy-dispersive spectroscopy (STEM-EDS) mappings demonstrate the uniform dispersion of C, N, O and Fe in $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ -MIL-101 (Fig. 1g). Moreover, the powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns suggest that the MIL-101, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101$ and $\mathrm{NO}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{MIL}..101$ share a similar topology (Fig. 1h). Also, compared with the Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) spectra of MIL-101, the presence of characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and $-\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101$ and $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ -MIL-101, respectively, verify that the $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and $-\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ have been successfully introduced into the MIL-101 (Fig. 1i). Moreover, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed to further verify the elemental compositions of the resultant MOFs. The peaks of C 1s, O 1s and $\mathrm{Fe}~2\mathrm{p}$ were observed in the full-range XPS spectra (Fig. S1a). The significant peak of N 1s in the XPS spectra manifests the existence of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ $(399.67\mathrm{eV})$
and $-\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ $(405.52\ \mathrm{eV})$ , which were well consistent with the observation from FT-IR (Fig. S1b) [46, 47].
## 3.2  POD‑Like Activity of MOFs
The introduction of the functional groups in MIL-101 was expected to rationally tune the POD-like activity. Based on the same content of metal active sites (Fe) (Table S1), a typical chromogenic reaction was conducted, in which the colorless $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) was oxidized to the blue oxidation state of TMB (oxTMB) in the presence of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ and nanozymes (Fig. 2a). It is found that the introduction of electron-withdrawing $-\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ enhances the POD-like activity of MIL-101, while the introduction of electrondonating $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ weakens the POD-like activity of MIL-101 (Fig. 2b). The specific activity (SA) was used to quantitatively evaluate the catalytic activity of these nanozymes under the optimal condition $(\mathrm{pH}=3.0$ , Fig. S2) [48]. Figure 2c shows that the SA of the $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ -MIL-101 and MIL-101 is 7.91- and 2.77-fold higher than that of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101, respectively. Then, the electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectroscopy verified the existence of hydroxyl radicals (·OH) (Fig. 2d), and the intensities of the signal peaks reflecting the relative content of ·OH are in good consistency with their POD-like activities [49]. The same conclusions were reached by using the degradation rates of methylene blue to indirectly monitoring the concentration of ·OH (Fig. S3) [50]. Moreover, the catalytic activities of these nanozymes were dramatically decreased upon the addition of isopropanol, which is a scavenger agent for ·OH [51], indicating that the ·OH was the main active intermediate (Fig. 2e). Then, the steady-state kinetics of these nanozymes was investigated to probe the enhanced POD-like activity of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ -MIL-101 (Fig. S4-S6). The Michaelis–Menten constant $(K_{m})$ value of $\mathrm{NO}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{MIL}.-101$ for ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ is lower than that of the other nanozymes, indicating the $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ -MIL-101 has a higher affinity toward ${\bf H}_{2}{\bf O}_{2}$ than that of the other nanozymes (Fig. 2f). Furthermore, the $V_{\mathrm{max}}$ values of $\mathrm{NO}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{MIL}.-101$ for both ${\bf H}_{2}{\bf O}_{2}$ and TMB are the highest among these nanozymes (Table S2). Based on these results, the introduction of $-\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ is noteworthy to improve the POD-like activity of MIL-101, while the introduction of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ exhibits the opposite effect. Notably, the excellent thermostability and reproducibility of the three MOFs were further verified, holding great promise for practical applications (Fig. S7).
